---
/**
 * Everything below the first screenful of the focus page.
 *
 * The game is the hero, and a hero made of canvas says nothing to a reader who
 * cannot see it, to someone deciding whether to trust the site, or to a search
 * engine. This is the page's actual content: what the thing is, how to use it,
 * why it works, and who it is for.
 *
 * Written answer-first. The heading asks the question a person would type and
 * the first sentence answers it outright, because that is what an assistant
 * quoting this page will lift, and it is also simply the clearest way to write.
 *
 * On the ADHD section: nothing here is a medical claim and nothing here is
 * advice. It describes what a timer does and what people report finding useful,
 * and says plainly that it is not a treatment. That is both honest and what
 * Google's policies on health content require of a page carrying ads.
 */
import { SPECIES, SPECIES_IDS } from '../three/species';
import { PET_ITEMS, SCENE_ITEMS } from '../game/economy';

/** One character per species, so the roster reads as animals not as SKUs. */
const cast = SPECIES_IDS.map((id) => ({
  species: SPECIES[id],
  character: PET_ITEMS.find((p) => p.species === id),
})).filter((c) => c.character);
---

<section class="pp-landing" aria-labelledby="what-is">
  <div class="pp-landing-inner">
    <header>
      <h1 id="what-is">A pomodoro timer with a pet that waits for you</h1>
      <p class="pp-lede">
        PetPomo is a free online pomodoro timer where a virtual pet lives in a hand-painted world
        that follows your real time of day, your local weather and the season outside your window.
        Start a 25-minute session and your pet curls up to sleep beside you. Finish it and it wakes,
        stretches, and brings you something. Nothing to install, no account, and it keeps working
        with the internet switched off.
      </p>
    </header>

    <h2>How to use it</h2>
    <ol class="pp-steps">
      <li><strong>Pick one thing to work on.</strong> One task per session — the technique falls apart when a session has three.</li>
      <li><strong>Press Start.</strong> Your pet settles down to sleep for 25 minutes. The countdown also runs in the browser tab, so you can watch it from another window.</li>
      <li><strong>Work until the bell.</strong> If you get distracted, write the distraction on a piece of paper and carry on. Chasing it is the thing that costs you the session.</li>
      <li><strong>Take the break properly.</strong> Stroke your pet until it purrs, drag a snack onto the ground to feed it, or tap it to play. Five minutes away from the screen is the point of the five minutes.</li>
      <li><strong>Every fourth session, take a longer break.</strong> Fifteen minutes. Stand up.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Focused minutes earn coins, and coins buy scenes, themes and new animals in the{' '}
      <a href="/shop">shop</a>. You can change the session lengths, move or resize the clock, pick its
      typeface, and turn the weather sounds down in <a href="/settings">settings</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What is the Pomodoro Technique?</h2>
    <p>
      Work for 25 minutes, rest for 5, and take a longer break after four rounds. That is the whole
      method. It was named after a tomato-shaped kitchen timer in the late 1980s, and it survives
      because it is small enough to actually do: it asks you to start, not to concentrate, and
      starting is usually the hard part.
    </p>
    <p>
      The 25 minutes matter less than the fact that they are <em>bounded</em>. A task with no end is
      something to avoid; a task with a bell at the end of it is something you can begin.
    </p>

    <h2>Does a pomodoro timer help with ADHD?</h2>
    <p>
      Many people with ADHD say they work better against an external clock than against their own
      sense of time, and a visible countdown is one of the most commonly suggested ways to make time
      feel concrete. A pomodoro timer gives a task a visible edge, a start you can press, and a
      finish that arrives whether or not the work went well.
    </p>
    <p>
      A pet adds a second thing that is easy to underrate: something is waiting on the other side of
      the session. A break with a small ritual in it — feed, stroke, play — is easier to actually
      take than a break that is just an absence of work, and taking breaks is the half of this
      method most people skip.
    </p>
    <p class="pp-note">
      PetPomo is a timer, not a treatment. It is not medical advice and it is not a substitute for
      speaking to a doctor or a specialist about ADHD or anything else.
    </p>

    <h2>Why a pet, and why it gets harder to abandon</h2>
    <p>
      A plain timer is a number going down. Nothing about it changes if you use it every day for a
      month, and nothing about it notices when you stop.
    </p>
    <p>
      Your pet does. It gets hungry while you are away and settles when you come back. It has a mood
      you can read at a glance — content, happy, affectionate, grumpy, sad, or unwell — and that mood
      is the sum of how you have treated it, not a random number. Look after it and it is delighted
      to see you. Disappear for two days and it is genuinely out of sorts when you return, and
      putting that right takes more than one biscuit.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of that makes you focus. What it does is give the habit somewhere to accumulate. After a
      few weeks the sessions stop being a thing you make yourself do and start being a thing you and
      something else do together, and that is a much easier thing to keep.
    </p>

    <h2>An aesthetic pomodoro timer that changes with your day</h2>
    <p>
      The world is painted, not photographed, and it is never quite the same twice. It runs on your
      device's clock, so dawn is dawn where you are, and it moves through six times of day into a
      night with real stars. It reads your local weather and paints it: cloud, fog, rain you can
      hear, snow, and storms with distant lightning. And it follows the season, the right way round
      for your hemisphere — a December session is midwinter in London and high summer in Sydney.
    </p>
    <ul class="pp-scenes">
      {SCENE_ITEMS.map((s) => (
        <li><strong>{s.name}</strong> — {s.blurb}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <h2>The animals</h2>
    <p>
      Every animal is built and animated in the browser rather than drawn as a sprite, which is why
      each one moves like itself: a horse walks on long legs and a bear does not, and a dog's tail
      sits up where a cat's sits level. They sound like themselves too — the bark, the meow, the
      bleat and the moo are all generated from the same synthesiser with different settings, and how
      often you hear them depends on the animal's mood and the hour. Nothing says much at 3am.
    </p>
    <ul class="pp-cast">
      {cast.map(({ species, character }) => (
        <li>
          <strong>{character!.name}</strong>
          <span class="pp-cast-kind">{species.label}</span>
          <span class="pp-cast-blurb">{character!.blurb}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <h2>Common questions</h2>
    <dl class="pp-faq">
      <dt>Is PetPomo free?</dt>
      <dd>Yes, completely. Coins are earned by focusing and can only be spent inside the game.</dd>

      <dt>Do I need an account?</dt>
      <dd>
        No. Your progress lives in your browser. Optional cloud sync uses a random code your own
        browser generates, so the server stores a save without ever learning who you are.
      </dd>

      <dt>Does it work offline?</dt>
      <dd>
        Yes. After your first visit the whole game is cached and runs with no connection. Only the
        live weather needs the network, and without it the world shows fair weather.
      </dd>

      <dt>How does it know my weather without asking permission?</dt>
      <dd>
        It uses the approximate location our host already attaches to every web request, rounded to
        about eleven kilometres before it is used. Your device location is never requested and no
        permission prompt appears.
      </dd>

      <dt>Can I use it for studying?</dt>
      <dd>
        That is most of what it is for. Set the focus length to match your attention rather than the
        default — 25 minutes is a starting point, not a rule — and use the long break to leave the
        desk.
      </dd>

      <dt>What happens if I abandon a session?</dt>
      <dd>
        It is not counted in your stats, and your pet is briefly sad about it. That is the entire
        penalty. Abandoning a session you were never going to finish is a reasonable thing to do.
      </dd>
    </dl>

    <p class="pp-landing-more">
      More on the method and the thinking behind it on the <a href="/blog">blog</a>, or read about how
      the world is built on the <a href="/about">about page</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
